This is not a library. It is its name, and the name is the point.

SHELL32.DLL held the desktop: the folder windows, the file dialogs, the
Start menu, the drag and drop, and the icons - all of them, in one file, at
fixed positions, which is why "the icon at index 4 in SHELL32.DLL" was
something people knew by heart and typed into shortcut dialogs.

Replacing this file replaced the desktop. Replacing it with the wrong
version replaced the desktop with a machine that started and then stopped.

On this system the desktop is a module resolved by a version tag, the icons
are PNG files in a folder you can open on C:, and nothing is addressed by
its position inside a binary. If the shell is replaced by a version that
does not work, the lock file still names the commit of the one that did.

Nothing opens a .DLL, which is precisely what a .DLL was for.
